- Confirm the recording shows before-style, cycle-urgent-command-start, after-urgent, cycle-calm-command-start, after-calm, cycle-wrap-command-start, after-wrap, and final inspection states.
- Confirm `[[zone-styles]]` and the ergonomic binding `alt-y = 'cycle-zone-style Comms urgent calm'` are visible through the caption/config surface.
- Confirm the exact user command is `winmux cycle-zone-style Comms urgent calm` for all three command beats. Reject any proof or caption that uses `set-zone-style` for this slice.
- Confirm the visual change is on the Comms zone chrome/sidebar row, including the styled row swatch/tint, not the TextEdit document content, not a layout resize, and not a workspace switch.
- Confirm the state sequence is no style -> urgent red `#D3455B`, urgent -> calm blue `#3EA2FF`, then calm -> urgent wraparound.
- Confirm Reference and Work stay unstyled, and the same windows and active workspaces stay in the same zone ids across all three cycle commands.
- State that Slice 19 proves ergonomic runtime style cycling only. It does not prove visual zone editing, draggable dividers, snap/gesture behavior, style persistence across relaunch, or new app/tab-group binding semantics.
- Reject logs-only style proof, final-state-only proof, a missing wraparound beat, a style swatch/tint too subtle to see, command captions that omit the target zone or style ids, and any proof where the reviewer cannot distinguish cycling style chrome from layout/workspace changes.
EOF
            ;;
        slice-11c-zone-availability-sets)
            cat <<'EOF'
- Confirm the recording starts with Reference, Work, and Comms visible, with the sidebar enabled and `[[zone-availability-sets]]` exposed by a caption/config chip.
- Sample frames around `Run: winmux set-zone-style Comms urgent`, `Run: winmux use-zone-availability focus-only`, and `Run: winmux use-zone-availability communications`. Fail if Comms becomes urgent, disappears, or reappears before the matching command caption appears.
- Confirm the recording shows before-availability, urgent-command-start, before-hide-urgent, focus-only-command-start, after-focus-only, communications-command-start, during-communications-restore, and after-communications-restore states.
- Confirm `focus-only` hides both side zones and visibly expands Work/main; this must not be only a log line.
- Confirm `communications` restores Comms/right beside Work/main while Reference/left remains hidden.
- Confirm the Comms document/window and workspace return to the right zone, not a new workspace or a different zone.
- Distinguish the top sidebar `Zones` section from lower parked workspace rows: parked Reference/Comms workspace rows may remain listed while their zones are unavailable.
- Confirm the urgent red `#D3455B` style is visible before hide, during restore, and after restore. Use the color-sentinel rows and screenshots, but still inspect the media.
- Confirm captions expose exact user commands: `winmux use-zone-availability focus-only`, `winmux use-zone-availability communications`, and inspection via `winmux list-zones`.
- Reject logs-only proof, final-state-only proof, unclear availability-set labels, command captions that omit the set id, a restore where Comms returns unstyled, or any video where the viewer cannot tell whether the action is a named set versus a manual zone toggle.
EOF
            ;;
        *)
            cat <<'EOF'
- Confirm the recording visually demonstrates the stated WinMux behavior without relying only on logs.
- Confirm captions expose concrete WinMux user actions, commands, or config.
EOF
            ;;
    esac
